WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:02.250
Speaker 1: I love segments like this.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:07.160
Next one today, we're going to go through
some examples of lying with graphs.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.360
I've told you before all the different ways
you can lie with statistics.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.140
There's a book I've recommended many times.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.470
It's called How to Lie with Statistics by
Daryl Huff.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:21.750
Not a new book, but a very important book
to understand in the book.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.930
It talks about sampling bias or selectively
choosing.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:29.970
When you say average choosing mean median
or mode, depending on which is most convenient

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.230
for the point you're trying to make.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:35.260
It talks about post hoc ergo propter hoc.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:41.109
All of this stuff is in the book, but one
of the chapters is called The Gee Whiz Graph.

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:48.409
It's a way to lie with graphs and a classic
in line with graphs specifically is truncating

00:00:48.409 --> 00:00:52.100
the Y axis or sometimes the X axis.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.690
The Y axis is the vertical axis on a graph.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:59.109
So think through just a general example and
then we'll look at what Fox News did in the

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:00.389
last few days.

00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:05.100
Imagine I want to give you the impression
that the temperature just skyrocketed when

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.189
it really didn't.

00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:09.549
Now, in the northeast of the United States,
over the course of a year, there's a really

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:10.760
broad temperature range.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.770
You know, in winter, there's some days where
it's minus five Fahrenheit.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.520
Sometimes you'll get a minus 10 Fahrenheit
day in some parts of the northeast.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.039
And yet in the summer, it can be like 100
hundred degrees Fahrenheit on a really hot

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:23.140
day during a heat wave.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.649
So that's a big range, minus 10 to 100.

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:31.859
If one day it's 72 degrees and the next day
it's 75 degrees, that's not a big change.

00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:36.810
When you think about sometimes it's minus
10, sometimes it's 100.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:44.829
But if I plot the 72 and 75 degree days and
really shorten that y axis, so the chart starts

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:52.299
at 70 degrees and ends at 80 degrees, that
three degree change between 72 and 75 will

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:54.600
look absolutely huge.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.409
So that gets us to Fox News these last few
days.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:02.290
And we have multiple examples here the other
day in one of his segments about how the United

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:07.640
States is, I guess, going down the tubes because
not enough people are religious anymore.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.400
Tucker Carlson, put up this graph, which we're
putting up on the screen, which shows the

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.640
percentage of Christians in the U.S. in 2009
and the percentage of Christians in the U.S.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:18.640
in 2019.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.930
And if you look at these yellow bars, that
looks like a big decline.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:29.620
But if you look at the y axis, you see that
this only starts at 58 percent and ends at

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.620
78 percent.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:37.129
Now, of course, anywhere from zero to 100
percent of Americans could identify as Christian.

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:38.569
That's the range.

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:46.019
But the chart makes the decline look huge
by only showing you the scale, 58 to 78.

00:02:46.019 --> 00:02:51.200
Now, Christianity is in decline of religiosity,
is in decline in the United States, but it's

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.769
not nearly as dramatic as this chart makes
it look to the untrained eye.

00:02:54.769 --> 00:02:56.430
Now, here's one other example.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.260
This is not truncating the y axis.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.200
It's sort of shortening the time horizon,
kind of like shortening the x axis in this

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.200
case.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.019
This was on the news side of Fox News, not
opinion.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:11.110
Bret Bhaya, news man Bret Bayer showed graphs
that make it look like the cost of oil and

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.400
gas is just way, way up under Joe Biden.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:21.890
So they showed this graph of oil prices and
it shows that oil and gas sure seem to have

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:28.640
been really low in 2020 under Trump and then,
boom, skyrocketing under Joe Biden.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.939
But the issue is it only looks at twenty nineteen
through what we have so far of twenty twenty

00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:35.159
one.

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:41.430
Then Bret shows this graph of gas prices,
which also shows only 2019 to 2021, and it

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.530
looks like gas prices went down in 2020 and
then exploded in twenty twenty one under Joe

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:47.530
Biden.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:48.940
Now this is deceptive for two reasons.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.640
The first is the pandemic.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.950
The pandemic hit and cratered demand for oil
and gas since people stopped traveling.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:58.890
And then the recovery started.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:02.280
And so demand went up and prices started going
up.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.129
That's one reason why it's deceptive.

00:04:04.129 --> 00:04:06.329
That's a qualitative reason.

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:08.239
But quantitatively, the.

00:04:08.239 --> 00:04:12.140
This is really deceptive because oil prices
have not.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.600
Oil prices have been going up for about seven
years, but they are still historically not

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:17.740
very high.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:22.390
When you zoom out and you look at a chart
of oil prices that goes back to like 1995

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:29.950
or so, you very quickly see that prices skyrocketed
around 2007 and then came down and then up

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.540
in 2012 and then came down.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:36.480
And now they're roughly at a seven year high
that started about seven years ago.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.190
It is not a Joe Biden thing.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:44.910
So when you zoom out, you see oil prices are
nowhere near record highs.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:51.620
And number two, they've been on an increasing
trend that is seven years old now, not this

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.940
year under Biden, very similar with natural
gas.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:02.810
You zoom out to 1995, you see gas prices were
higher in 2001 and 2006 and 2009.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.720
And, yes, they're up again now, extremely
deceptive.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.620
Always look at the labels on the acces.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.040
This is stuff every 12 year old should be
taught.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.130
And if we started teaching this one when kids
were 12, way fewer people would fall for it.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.130
On Fox News.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:06:20.800
We'll have these graphs and more examples
on our Instagram, which you can find 

